<details class="optional" id="before"><summary>Before the first Azure run</summary>
<p>This is a participant-path checklist, not an instructor timetable. Record actual outcomes, elapsed time, region, selected versions and fixes. The proposed core duration is an estimate until a person follows it successfully.</p>
<ol><li>Build the static site with <code>python scripts/build.py</code>. Serve the repository at the documented base path and follow the workshop link from the main page. Check navigation, copy, search, source viewer, theme and persisted completion/reset.</li><li>Use a clean, suitable subscription scope or a fresh workshop prefix. Run prerequisite checks without bypassing missing permissions or quota. Confirm the actual selected subscription with the owner.</li><li>Review the cost estimate, create only the model stage, and require the actual identity-based embedding/chat probe to pass before AKS/database provisioning starts.</li><li>Start stage 02, then perform the Docker chapter while provisioning runs. Record whether this overlap is enough to keep the main path near four hours.</li><li>Wait through stage 03. Verify the VM is Ready, Functions is on Flex/Python 3.12, PostgreSQL has private networking and VECTOR is allowlisted. No internet-wide database firewall rule should exist.</li><li>Complete roles/settings/federation. Inspect the KEDA operator's identity separately, and confirm the kubelet can pull both image tags.</li></ol>
</details>
<details class="optional" id="acceptance"><summary>Core acceptance observations</summary>
<table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Expected outcome</th><th>Useful diagnostic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Products rollout</td><td>Ready Pod, Flyway version 1, signed-in upload/chat pages over restricted HTTPS</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod -n products -l app=products</code>; Products logs</td></tr><tr><td>New manual</td><td>Waiting/processing → ready; nonzero unique chunks, 1536 dimensions</td><td><code>bash workshop/scripts/inspect-data.sh</code>; Function manual-ID trace</td></tr><tr><td>Duplicate event</td><td>No extra chunk rows or changed ready data</td><td><code>python workshop/tools/replay_event.py</code>, then compare counts</td></tr><tr><td>Known question</td><td>Queued/processing → completed; correct correlation and relevant product/manual references</td><td>Request ID in worker/Products logs and PostgreSQL state</td></tr><tr><td>Unsupported question</td><td>Explicit insufficient information, no invented specification</td><td>Read actual retrieved passages and model instruction</td></tr><tr><td>Retrieval setting</td><td>After worker restart, new request uses count=1; restore 3</td><td>Worker startup log and at most one returned citation</td></tr><tr><td>KEDA cycle</td><td>0 → backlog → 1 → more than 1 → completed answers → 0; Products stays 1</td><td>Observer output, ScaledObject/HPA conditions, model throttle logs</td></tr><tr><td>Real smoke</td><td>Pass with source IDs and temperature evidence, without exact answer assertion</td><td><code>.state/smoke-result.json</code>; bounded timeout diagnostic</td></tr><tr><td>Cleanup</td><td>Workshop group and managed AKS node group gone; retention explained</td><td>Printed <code>az group exists</code> command and cost/resource view</td></tr></tbody></table>
</details>
<details class="optional" id="local-tests"><summary>Reproduce local software checks</summary>
<p>{{file:scripts/verify-local.sh}} runs local verification with a disposable loopback-only PostgreSQL/pgvector container and removes that container on exit. It requires Java 21, Docker and Python 3.12+. Its model fixtures are deterministic: passing them does not demonstrate Azure identity or live model behavior.</p>
<p>Run <code>bash workshop/scripts/verify-local.sh</code>. Use <code>python -m playwright install chromium</code> inside the verification environment before the browser suite, or set <code>BROWSER_EXECUTABLE</code> to a compatible installed Chromium/Edge executable. The browser tests run at <code>/ai200/workshop/</code>, not only at the host root.</p>
<p>For infrastructure, compile all four Bicep files with <code>for file in workshop/infra/*.bicep; do az bicep build --file "$file" --stdout &gt; /dev/null; done</code> and perform ARM/server-side validation in the intended subscription before a clean deployment. Run <code>bash -n</code> and ShellCheck on the scripts. Validate rendered standard manifests against Kubernetes schemas and KEDA resources against the official add-on CRDs; then validate against the actual cluster with <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> before applying.</p>
<p>The supplied <code>python workshop/tools/validate_manifests.py</code> creates non-secret fixture renderings and validates the custom resources against official KEDA 2.18.2 CRDs. Standard resources can be checked with the pinned kubeconform container, as reproduced in the repository's workshop-checks workflow. These offline structures do not prove live cluster admission, current Azure capacity or identity propagation.</p>
</details>
<details class="optional" id="record"><summary>Record what actually happened</summary>
<p>Update {{file:VALIDATION.md}} with command summaries and dates. Separate local fixture results, container results, ARM/schema checks, live model probes and the actual deployment smoke result. Do not turn “expected output” from a chapter into an observed result. Record any Azure blocker explicitly and leave its acceptance row unverified.</p>
<p>If the path takes substantially longer than four hours after prerequisites, record the slow chapters. Keep advanced indexing, identity hardening and production operations optional; do not remove the requested core learning goals just to claim a shorter duration.</p>
</details>
